<section xml:id="section-conics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Conic Sections</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Conic sections</term> are the curves obtained by intersecting a double
      cone with a plane. There are three types: <term>parabolas</term>,
      <term>ellipses</term>, and <term>hyperbolas</term>. In this section we give the
      focus<ndash/>directrix and focal definitions of each, read the key features off the
      standard equations, and see a worked example together with a second example that
      changes the orientation.
    </p>

    <interactive xml:id="interactive-conic-slicer" iframe="conic-slicer.html" width="100%" aspect="8:7">
      <instructions>
        <p>
        The surface is the double cone <m>x^2 + y^2 = z^2</m>, cut by the plane
        <m>z = m\,y + c</m>. Use the buttons to jump to the three cases, or drag the
        <q>tilt</q> slider yourself and watch the red curve change from an ellipse
        (<m>m \lt 1</m>, plane shallower than the slant) to a parabola (<m>m = 1</m>,
        plane parallel to the slant) to a hyperbola (<m>m \gt 1</m>, plane steep enough
        to cut both nappes). The <q>offset</q> slider moves the plane up and down; try
        setting <m>c = 0</m> so the plane passes through the apex and the section
        degenerates to a point, one line, or a pair of crossing lines. Drag the figure
        to view it from a different angle, or press the <q>Rotate</q> button to spin it
        automatically.</p>
      </instructions>
    </interactive>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ===================== PARABOLA ===================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-parabola">
    <title>Parabola</title>
    <p>
      A <term>parabola</term> is the set of points in a plane that are equidistant from a
      fixed point <m>F</m> (the <term>focus</term>) and a fixed line (the
      <term>directrix</term>). For the standard parabola <m>x^2 = 4py</m> the vertex is at
      the origin, the focus is at <m>(0,p)</m>, and the directrix is the line <m>y = -p</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-parabola-def">
      <caption>The parabola <m>x^2 = 4py</m>. The point <m>P</m> is equidistant from the
      focus <m>(0,p)</m> and the directrix <m>y=-p</m>.</caption>
      <image width="65%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="parabola-def">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,246)" margins="8">
            <definition>pval = 0.6</definition>
            <definition>f(x) = x^2/(4*pval)</definition>
            <definition>xP = 1.9</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.6, -1.15, 2.6, 2.05)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
              <graph at="parabola" function="f" domain="(-2.25, 2.25)" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <line at="axis-sym" p1="(0,-pval)" p2="(0,1.95)" dash="3 3" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
              <line at="directrix" p1="(-2.45,-pval)" p2="(2.45,-pval)" stroke="red" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
              <label at="dir-label" anchor="(-2.45,-pval)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,4)"><m>\text{directrix: } y=-p</m></label>
              <point at="focus" p="(0,pval)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="focus-label" anchor="(0,pval)" alignment="w" offset="(-8,4)"><m>\text{Focus }(0,p)</m></label>
              <point at="vertex" p="(0,0)" fill="orange" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="vertex-label" anchor="(0,0)" alignment="se" offset="(6,-2)"><m>\text{vertex}</m></label>
              <group at="equidistant">
                <point p="(xP,f(xP))" fill="blue" size="3"/>
                <label anchor="(xP,f(xP))" alignment="ne" offset="(4,2)"><m>P</m></label>
                <line p1="(xP,f(xP))" p2="(0,pval)" stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
                <line p1="(xP,f(xP))" p2="(xP,-pval)" stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
                <point p="(xP,-pval)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
              </group>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(-1.15,1.5)" alignment="center"><m>x^2 = 4py</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="parabola"
                  text="A parabola opening upward with vertex at the origin, illustrating the defining property that every point is equidistant from a fixed focus and a fixed directrix">
                <annotation ref="parabola"
                    text="The parabola x squared equals four p y, shown as a blue upward curve" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="focus"
                    text="The focus, the fixed point at zero comma p on the axis of symmetry"/>
                <annotation ref="directrix"
                    text="The directrix, the fixed horizontal line y equals minus p, drawn dashed in red"/>
                <annotation ref="vertex"
                    text="The vertex at the origin, the point of the parabola halfway between the focus and the directrix"/>
                <annotation ref="equidistant"
                    text="A point P on the parabola with two equal green segments, one to the focus and one dropped perpendicular to the directrix, showing the two distances are equal"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-parabola-focus">
      <caption>Animation: as <m>P</m> moves along the parabola, its distance to the focus
      always equals its distance to the directrix.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-parabola-focus"
             source="videos/parabola_focus_directrix_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-parabola-1">
      <title>From equation to focus and directrix</title>
      <p>Find the focus and the directrix of <m>y = -\frac{1}{2}x^2 + x - \frac{1}{2}</m>.</p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-parabola-1">
      <p>We begin by completing the square:</p>
      <me>y = -\frac{1}{2}x^2 + x - \frac{1}{2} = -\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^2,
      \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad (x-1)^2 = -2y.</me>
      <p>
        Comparing with <m>x^2 = 4py</m> gives <m>4p = -2</m>, so <m>p = -\frac{1}{2}</m>.
        The graph is the standard parabola shifted right by one unit, so the vertex is at
        <m>(1,0)</m>. Therefore the focus is at
      </p>
      <me>(1,\, p) = \left(1,\, -\tfrac{1}{2}\right),</me>
      <p>and the directrix is the horizontal line <m>y = -p = \tfrac{1}{2}</m>.</p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-parabola-example">
        <caption>The parabola <m>(x-1)^2 = -2y</m>, opening downward with focus
        <m>\left(1,-\tfrac12\right)</m> and directrix <m>y=\tfrac12</m>.</caption>
        <image width="65%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="parabola-example">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,296)" margins="8">
            <definition>g(x) = -0.5*(x-1)^2</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.35, -1.15, 2.35, 0.85)">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
              <graph at="parabola" function="g" domain="(-0.1, 2.1)" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <line at="directrix" p1="(-0.3,0.5)" p2="(2.3,0.5)" stroke="red" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
              <label at="dir-label" anchor="(1.6,0.5)" alignment="n" offset="(0,6)"><m>\text{directrix } y=\tfrac12</m></label>
              <point at="focus" p="(1,-0.5)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="focus-label" anchor="(1,-0.5)" alignment="se" offset="(6,-2)"><m>\text{Focus }\left(1,-\tfrac12\right)</m></label>
              <point at="vertex" p="(1,0)" fill="orange" size="3"/>
              <label at="curve-label" anchor="(0.5,-0.85)" alignment="e"><m>(x-1)^2=-2y</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="parabola"
                  text="The downward parabola of the worked example, obtained by completing the square, with a horizontal directrix above it and a focus below the vertex">
                <annotation ref="parabola"
                    text="The parabola x minus one squared equals minus two y, a blue curve opening downward with vertex at one comma zero" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="vertex"
                    text="The vertex at one comma zero, the highest point of this downward parabola"/>
                <annotation ref="focus"
                    text="The focus at one comma minus one half, located one half unit below the vertex"/>
                <annotation ref="directrix"
                    text="The directrix, the horizontal line y equals one half, one half unit above the vertex"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>

    <example xml:id="ex-parabola-2">
      <title>From focus and directrix to equation (a sideways parabola)</title>
      <p>
        Find the equation of the parabola whose focus is <m>(2,0)</m> and whose directrix
        is the vertical line <m>x = -2</m>. In which direction does it open?
      </p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-parabola-2">
      <p>
        Here we run the definition in reverse. A point <m>(x,y)</m> lies on the parabola
        exactly when its distance to the focus equals its distance to the directrix:
      </p>
      <me>\sqrt{(x-2)^2 + y^2} = |x+2|.</me>
      <p>Squaring both sides and expanding,</p>
      <me>(x-2)^2 + y^2 = (x+2)^2
      \;\Longrightarrow\; x^2 - 4x + 4 + y^2 = x^2 + 4x + 4.</me>
      <p>The <m>x^2</m> and constant terms cancel, leaving</p>
      <me>y^2 = 8x.</me>
      <p>
        Because the directrix is <em>vertical</em> and the focus lies to its right, the
        parabola opens <em>to the right</em>. Comparing with <m>y^2 = 4px</m> gives
        <m>4p = 8</m>, so <m>p = 2</m>, consistent with the focus at <m>(2,0)</m>. This is
        the same equidistance idea as the previous example, but with a horizontal axis of
        symmetry.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-parabola-extra">
        <caption>The parabola <m>y^2 = 8x</m> opens to the right; <m>P</m> is equidistant
        from the focus <m>(2,0)</m> and the directrix <m>x=-2</m>.</caption>
        <image width="65%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="parabola-extra">
          <diagram dimensions="(296,340)" margins="8">
            <definition>P(x,y) = y^2 - 8*x</definition>
            <definition>yP = 4</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.8, -4.6, 5.2, 4.6)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
              <implicit-curve at="parabola" function="P" k="0" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <line at="directrix" p1="(-2,-4.4)" p2="(-2,4.4)" stroke="red" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
              <label at="dir-label" anchor="(-2,-3.6)" alignment="e" offset="(4,0)"><m>x=-2</m></label>
              <point at="focus" p="(2,0)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="focus-label" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="se" offset="(6,-4)"><m>\text{Focus }(2,0)</m></label>
              <point at="vertex" p="(0,0)" fill="orange" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="vertex-label" anchor="(0,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-4,-4)"><m>\text{vertex}</m></label>
              <group at="equidistant">
                <point p="(yP^2/8, yP)" fill="blue" size="3"/>
                <label at="pt-P-label" anchor="(yP^2/8, yP)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,2)"><m>P</m></label>
                <line p1="(yP^2/8, yP)" p2="(2,0)" stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
                <line p1="(yP^2/8, yP)" p2="(-2, yP)" stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
                <point p="(-2, yP)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
              </group>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(3.4,-3.2)" alignment="center"><m>y^2 = 8x</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="parabola"
                  text="A sideways parabola opening to the right, built from the definition using a focus and a vertical directrix, the twist example that reverses the usual direction">
                <annotation ref="parabola"
                    text="The parabola y squared equals eight x, a blue curve opening to the right with vertex at the origin" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="focus"
                    text="The focus at two comma zero, to the right of the vertex"/>
                <annotation ref="directrix"
                    text="The directrix, the vertical line x equals minus two, drawn dashed in red to the left of the vertex"/>
                <annotation ref="vertex"
                    text="The vertex at the origin, halfway between the focus and the directrix"/>
                <annotation ref="equidistant"
                    text="A point P on the parabola with two equal green segments, one to the focus and one horizontal to the directrix, confirming the equal distances"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ===================== ELLIPSE ===================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ellipse">
    <title>Ellipse</title>
    <p>
      An <term>ellipse</term> is the set of points in a plane the sum of whose distances
      from two fixed points <m>F_1</m> and <m>F_2</m> (the <term>foci</term>) is constant.
      For <m>\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1</m> with <m>a \gt b</m>, the segment
      joining <m>(a,0)</m> and <m>(-a,0)</m> is the <term>semi-major axis</term>, the
      segment joining <m>(0,b)</m> and <m>(0,-b)</m> is the <term>semi-minor axis</term>,
      and the foci satisfy <m>c^2 = a^2 - b^2 \gt 0</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ellipse-def">
      <caption>An ellipse with foci <m>F_1(c,0)</m> and <m>F_2(-c,0)</m>. Each slanted
      segment from <m>(0,b)</m> to a focus has length <m>a</m>.</caption>
      <image width="65%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ellipse-def">
          <diagram dimensions="(420,307)" margins="8">
            <definition>aa = 3.2</definition>
            <definition>bb = 2.2</definition>
            <definition>cc = sqrt(aa^2 - bb^2)</definition>
            <definition>E(x,y) = x^2/aa^2 + y^2/bb^2</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-4.1, -3.0, 4.1, 3.0)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
              <implicit-curve at="ellipse" function="E" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <group at="focal-radii">
                <line p1="(0,bb)" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
                <line p1="(0,bb)" p2="(-cc,0)" stroke="orange" thickness="2"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(cc/2,bb/2)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,2)"><m>a</m></label>
              <label anchor="(-cc/2,bb/2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,2)"><m>a</m></label>
              <group at="foci">
                <point p="(cc,0)" fill="red" size="3"/>
                <point p="(-cc,0)" fill="red" size="3"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-6)"><m>F_1(c,0)</m></label>
              <label anchor="(-cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-6)"><m>F_2(-c,0)</m></label>
              <point at="cov-top" p="(0,bb)" fill="green" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(0,bb)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,2)"><m>(0,b)</m></label>
              <label anchor="(0,-bb)" alignment="se" offset="(4,-2)"><m>(0,-b)</m></label>
              <label at="vx-r" anchor="(aa,0)" alignment="n" offset="(-34,6)"><m>(a,0)</m></label>
              <label at="vx-l" anchor="(-aa,0)" alignment="n" offset="(34,6)"><m>(-a,0)</m></label>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(-2.7,2.2)" alignment="center"><m>\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="ellipse"
                  text="A horizontal ellipse illustrating the definition of an ellipse as the set of points whose distances to two foci have a constant sum">
                <annotation ref="ellipse"
                    text="The ellipse x squared over a squared plus y squared over b squared equals one, a blue oval wider than it is tall" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="foci"
                    text="The two foci F one at c comma zero and F two at minus c comma zero, both on the x axis inside the ellipse"/>
                <annotation ref="focal-radii"
                    text="Two segments drawn from the top co-vertex zero comma b to each focus; each has length a, showing that their sum equals two a"/>
                <annotation ref="cov-top"
                    text="The top co-vertex at zero comma b, the end of the semi-minor axis"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-ellipse-sum">
      <caption>Animation: as <m>P</m> travels around the ellipse, the sum <m>r_1 + r_2</m>
      of its distances to the two foci stays fixed at <m>2a = 6</m>.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-ellipse-sum"
             source="videos/ellipse_constant_sum_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Where do the formulas for <m>a</m> come from? Two facts do all the work. First,
      evaluating the definition at the vertex <m>(a,0)</m> shows that the two focal radii
      there are <m>a-c</m> and <m>a+c</m>, so their sum is
    </p>
    <me>r_1 + r_2 = (a-c) + (a+c) = 2a,</me>
    <p>
      which identifies the constant in the definition as <m>2a</m>; equivalently
      <m>a = \frac{1}{2}(r_1 + r_2)</m>. Second, evaluating at the co-vertex <m>(0,b)</m>
      forces <m>r_1 = r_2 = a</m> by symmetry, and that radius is the hypotenuse of a right
      triangle with legs <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>. Pythagoras then gives
    </p>
    <me>a^2 = b^2 + c^2 \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad c^2 = a^2 - b^2.</me>
    <p>
      So for an ellipse <m>a</m> is the <em>hypotenuse</em> of that triangle, which is why
      <m>a</m> is the largest of the three lengths and why <m>c^2 = a^2 - b^2</m> carries a
      minus sign.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="vid-ellipse-formula-a">
      <caption>Animation: deriving the formula for <m>a</m>. Sliding <m>P</m> to the vertex
      shows the constant equals <m>2a</m>; sliding it to the co-vertex builds the right
      triangle giving <m>a^2 = b^2 + c^2</m>.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-ellipse-formula-a"
             source="videos/ellipse_formula_a_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-ellipse-find-a">
      <caption>Animation: in practice, put the equation in standard form and read <m>a^2</m>
      off the <em>larger</em> denominator; then <m>a</m> is the semi-major axis, measured
      from the center to a vertex.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-ellipse-find-a"
             source="videos/ellipse_find_a_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-ellipse-1">
      <title>Horizontal ellipse</title>
      <p>Sketch the graph of <m>4x^2 + 9y^2 = 36</m> and locate the foci.</p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-ellipse-1">
      <p>Dividing through by <m>36</m> puts the equation in standard form:</p>
      <me>\frac{x^2}{9} + \frac{y^2}{4} = 1.</me>
      <p>
        The larger denominator sits under <m>x^2</m>, so the major axis is horizontal with
        <m>a = 3</m> and <m>b = 2</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me>c^2 = a^2 - b^2 = 9 - 4 = 5 \;\Longrightarrow\; c = \sqrt{5},</me>
      <p>so the foci are <m>F_1(\sqrt{5},0)</m> and <m>F_2(-\sqrt{5},0)</m>.</p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ellipse-example">
        <caption>The ellipse <m>\frac{x^2}{9}+\frac{y^2}{4}=1</m> with foci at
        <m>\left(\pm\sqrt5,\,0\right)</m>.</caption>
        <image width="65%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ellipse-example">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,292)" margins="8">
            <definition>E(x,y) = x^2/9 + y^2/4</definition>
            <definition>cc = sqrt(5)</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-3.7, -2.7, 3.7, 2.7)">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
              <implicit-curve at="ellipse" function="E" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <group at="focal-radii">
                <line p1="(0,2)" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="darkred" thickness="2"/>
                <line p1="(0,2)" p2="(-cc,0)" stroke="darkred" thickness="2"/>
              </group>
              <group at="foci">
                <point p="(cc,0)" fill="red" size="3"/>
                <point p="(-cc,0)" fill="red" size="3"/>
              </group>
              <label at="f1-label" anchor="(cc,0)" alignment="n" offset="(2,6)"><m>F_1</m></label>
              <label at="f2-label" anchor="(-cc,0)" alignment="n" offset="(-4,6)"><m>F_2</m></label>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(-2.35,2.1)" alignment="center"><m>\frac{x^2}{9}+\frac{y^2}{4}=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="ellipse"
                  text="The worked-example ellipse with a horizontal major axis and its two foci located on the x axis">
                <annotation ref="ellipse"
                    text="The ellipse x squared over nine plus y squared over four equals one, a blue oval with semi-major axis three and semi-minor axis two" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="foci"
                    text="The foci at plus and minus the square root of five on the x axis, since c squared equals nine minus four equals five"/>
                <annotation ref="focal-radii"
                    text="Two segments from the top of the ellipse to the two foci, illustrating the constant-sum property"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>

    <example xml:id="ex-ellipse-2">
      <title>A vertical ellipse (foci on the <m>y</m>-axis)</title>
      <p>Sketch the graph of <m>25x^2 + 16y^2 = 400</m> and locate the foci.</p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-ellipse-2">
      <p>Dividing by <m>400</m>,</p>
      <me>\frac{x^2}{16} + \frac{y^2}{25} = 1.</me>
      <p>
        This time the larger denominator, <m>25</m>, sits under <m>y^2</m>, so the major
        axis is <em>vertical</em>. The semi-major axis is <m>a = \sqrt{25} = 5</m> (along
        the <m>y</m>-axis) and the semi-minor axis is <m>b = \sqrt{16} = 4</m> (along the
        <m>x</m>-axis). Hence
      </p>
      <me>c^2 = a^2 - b^2 = 25 - 16 = 9 \;\Longrightarrow\; c = 3.</me>
      <p>
        Because the major axis is vertical, the foci lie on the <m>y</m>-axis:
        <m>F_1(0,3)</m> and <m>F_2(0,-3)</m>. The lesson of the twist: read off the larger
        denominator first, since it decides <em>which axis</em> carries the foci.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ellipse-extra">
        <caption>The vertical ellipse <m>\frac{x^2}{16}+\frac{y^2}{25}=1</m>; the foci
        <m>(0,\pm 3)</m> sit on the major axis, and each segment from <m>(4,0)</m> to a
        focus has length <m>a = 5</m>.</caption>
        <image width="55%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ellipse-extra">
          <diagram dimensions="(360,399)" margins="8">
            <definition>E(x,y) = x^2/16 + y^2/25</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-5.6, -6.2, 5.6, 6.2)">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
              <implicit-curve at="ellipse" function="E" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <group at="focal-radii">
                <line p1="(4,0)" p2="(0,3)" stroke="darkred" thickness="2"/>
                <line p1="(4,0)" p2="(0,-3)" stroke="darkred" thickness="2"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(2,1.5)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,2)"><m>a=5</m></label>
              <group at="foci">
                <point p="(0,3)" fill="red" size="3"/>
                <point p="(0,-3)" fill="red" size="3"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(0,3)" alignment="e" offset="(6,2)"><m>F_1(0,3)</m></label>
              <label anchor="(0,-3)" alignment="e" offset="(6,-2)"><m>F_2(0,-3)</m></label>
              <point at="vertex-top" p="(0,5)" fill="blue" size="2.5"/>
              <label anchor="(0,5)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,2)"><m>(0,5)</m></label>
              <label anchor="(4,0)" alignment="se" offset="(2,-4)"><m>(4,0)</m></label>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(-3.55,5.3)" alignment="center"><m>\frac{x^2}{16}+\frac{y^2}{25}=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="ellipse"
                  text="The twist example: a vertical ellipse whose major axis runs along the y axis, so its foci lie on the y axis rather than the x axis">
                <annotation ref="ellipse"
                    text="The ellipse x squared over sixteen plus y squared over twenty five equals one, a blue oval taller than it is wide" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="foci"
                    text="The foci at zero comma three and zero comma minus three, on the vertical major axis, since c squared equals twenty five minus sixteen equals nine"/>
                <annotation ref="vertex-top"
                    text="The top vertex at zero comma five, the end of the semi-major axis of length five"/>
                <annotation ref="focal-radii"
                    text="Two segments from the co-vertex four comma zero to each focus, each of length a equals five"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ===================== HYPERBOLA ===================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-hyperbola">
    <title>Hyperbola</title>
    <p>
      A <term>hyperbola</term> is the set of points in a plane the <em>difference</em> of
      whose distances from two fixed points <m>F_1</m> and <m>F_2</m> (the
      <term>foci</term>) is constant. For <m>\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1</m> the
      vertices are <m>(\pm a, 0)</m>, the foci satisfy <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>, and the
      asymptotes are <m>y = \pm\dfrac{b}{a}\,x</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-hyperbola-def">
      <caption>A hyperbola opening left and right, with vertices <m>(\pm a,0)</m>, foci
      <m>(\pm c,0)</m>, and asymptotes <m>y = \pm\frac{b}{a}x</m>.</caption>
      <image width="65%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="hyperbola-def">
          <diagram dimensions="(420,313)" margins="8">
            <definition>aa = 2</definition>
            <definition>bb = 1.6</definition>
            <definition>cc = sqrt(aa^2 + bb^2)</definition>
            <definition>H(x,y) = x^2/aa^2 - y^2/bb^2</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-4.3, -3.2, 4.3, 3.2)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
              <group at="asymptotes">
                <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(aa,bb)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
                <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(aa,-bb)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
              </group>
              <implicit-curve at="hyperbola" function="H" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <label at="asym-label-r" anchor="(3.0,3.0*bb/aa)" alignment="nw" offset="(-2,2)"><m>y=\tfrac{b}{a}x</m></label>
              <label at="asym-label-l" anchor="(-3.0,3.0*bb/aa)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,2)"><m>y=-\tfrac{b}{a}x</m></label>
              <group at="foci">
                <point p="(cc,0)" fill="green" size="3"/>
                <point p="(-cc,0)" fill="green" size="3"/>
              </group>
              <label at="f1-label" anchor="(cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(14,-6)"><m>F_1(c,0)</m></label>
              <label at="f2-label" anchor="(-cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(-14,-6)"><m>F_2(-c,0)</m></label>
              <group at="vertices">
                <point p="(aa,0)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
                <point p="(-aa,0)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
              </group>
              <label at="v1-label" anchor="(aa,0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)"><m>V_1</m></label>
              <label at="v2-label" anchor="(-aa,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)"><m>V_2</m></label>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(1.9,-2.7)" alignment="center"><m>\frac{x^2}{a^2}-\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="hyperbola"
                  text="A hyperbola opening left and right, illustrating the definition of a hyperbola as the set of points whose distances to two foci have a constant difference">
                <annotation ref="hyperbola"
                    text="The hyperbola x squared over a squared minus y squared over b squared equals one, two blue branches opening left and right" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="asymptotes"
                    text="The two dashed asymptotes through the origin, the lines y equals plus and minus b over a times x, which the branches approach"/>
                <annotation ref="vertices"
                    text="The vertices V one at a comma zero and V two at minus a comma zero, where each branch is closest to the center"/>
                <annotation ref="foci"
                    text="The foci F one at c comma zero and F two at minus c comma zero, beyond the vertices on the x axis"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-hyperbola-diff">
      <caption>Animation: as <m>P</m> moves along a branch, the absolute difference
      <m>|r_1 - r_2|</m> of its distances to the foci stays fixed at <m>2a = 8</m>, while
      the branch hugs its asymptotes.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-hyperbola-diff"
             source="videos/hyperbola_constant_difference_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The formulas for <m>a</m> arise exactly as they did for the ellipse, with sums
      replaced by differences. Evaluating the definition at the vertex <m>(a,0)</m>, the two
      focal radii are <m>c-a</m> and <m>c+a</m>, so
    </p>
    <me>|r_1 - r_2| = (c+a) - (c-a) = 2a,</me>
    <p>
      identifying the constant as <m>2a</m>, or <m>a = \frac{1}{2}\,|r_1 - r_2|</m>. For the
      second relation, draw the central box whose half-width is <m>a</m> and half-height is
      <m>b</m>. Its corner <m>(a,b)</m> lies at distance <m>c</m> from the center, so the
      right triangle with legs <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> has hypotenuse <m>c</m>, and
    </p>
    <me>c^2 = a^2 + b^2 \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad a^2 = c^2 - b^2.</me>
    <p>
      Notice the contrast with the ellipse: there <m>a</m> was the <em>hypotenuse</em>, so
      <m>c^2 = a^2 - b^2</m>; here <m>a</m> is a <em>leg</em>, so <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>.
      That single difference is the source of the sign change between the two formulas.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="vid-hyperbola-formula-a">
      <caption>Animation: deriving the formula for <m>a</m>. Sliding <m>P</m> to the vertex
      shows the constant equals <m>2a</m>; the central box then builds the right triangle
      giving <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-hyperbola-formula-a"
             source="videos/hyperbola_formula_a_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-hyperbola-find-a">
      <caption>Animation: in practice, put the equation in standard form and read <m>a^2</m>
      off the denominator under the <em>positive</em> term; then <m>a</m> is measured along
      the transverse axis, from the center to a vertex.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-hyperbola-find-a"
             source="videos/hyperbola_find_a_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-hyperbola-1">
      <title>Horizontal hyperbola</title>
      <p>Find the foci and asymptotes of the hyperbola <m>9x^2 - 16y^2 = 144</m> and sketch
      its graph.</p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-hyperbola-1">
      <p>Dividing by <m>144</m> gives standard form:</p>
      <me>\frac{x^2}{16} - \frac{y^2}{9} = 1
      \;\Longrightarrow\; a = \sqrt{16} = 4,\quad b = \sqrt{9} = 3.</me>
      <p>Since the <m>x^2</m>-term is positive, the hyperbola opens left and right. Then</p>
      <me>c^2 = a^2 + b^2 = 16 + 9 = 25 \;\Longrightarrow\; c = 5,</me>
      <p>
        so the foci are <m>(\pm 5, 0)</m>, the vertices are <m>(\pm 4, 0)</m>, and the
        asymptotes are <m>y = \pm\frac{b}{a}x = \pm\frac{3}{4}x</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-hyperbola-example">
        <caption>The hyperbola <m>\frac{x^2}{16}-\frac{y^2}{9}=1</m> with foci
        <m>(\pm5,0)</m>, vertices <m>(\pm4,0)</m>, and asymptotes <m>y=\pm\frac34 x</m>.</caption>
        <image width="70%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="hyperbola-example">
          <diagram dimensions="(434,340)" margins="8">
            <definition>H(x,y) = x^2/16 - y^2/9</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-11.5, -9, 11.5, 9)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1" hticks="(-10,5,10)" vticks="(-8,4,8)" tick-size="(4,4)"/>
              <group at="asymptotes">
                <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(4,3)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
                <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(4,-3)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
              </group>
              <implicit-curve at="hyperbola" function="H" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <label at="asym-r" anchor="(7,5.25)" alignment="nw"><m>y=\tfrac34 x</m></label>
              <label at="asym-l" anchor="(-7,5.25)" alignment="ne"><m>y=-\tfrac34 x</m></label>
              <group at="foci">
                <point p="(5,0)" fill="green" size="3"/>
                <point p="(-5,0)" fill="green" size="3"/>
              </group>
              <label at="f1-label" anchor="(5,0)" alignment="s" offset="(26,-8)"><m>F_1(5,0)</m></label>
              <label at="f2-label" anchor="(-5,0)" alignment="s" offset="(-26,-8)"><m>F_2(-5,0)</m></label>
              <group at="vertices">
                <point p="(4,0)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
                <point p="(-4,0)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
              </group>
              <label at="v1-label" anchor="(4,0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,8)"><m>V_1</m></label>
              <label at="v2-label" anchor="(-4,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,8)"><m>V_2</m></label>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(5.8,-7.6)" alignment="center"><m>\frac{x^2}{16}-\frac{y^2}{9}=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="hyperbola"
                  text="The worked-example hyperbola opening left and right, with vertices at plus and minus four, foci at plus and minus five, and asymptotes of slope three quarters">
                <annotation ref="hyperbola"
                    text="The hyperbola x squared over sixteen minus y squared over nine equals one, two blue branches opening left and right" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="asymptotes"
                    text="The dashed asymptotes y equals plus and minus three quarters x through the origin"/>
                <annotation ref="vertices"
                    text="The vertices at plus and minus four on the x axis, where a equals four"/>
                <annotation ref="foci"
                    text="The foci at plus and minus five on the x axis, since c squared equals sixteen plus nine equals twenty five"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>

    <example xml:id="ex-hyperbola-2">
      <title>A vertical hyperbola (opens up and down)</title>
      <p>Find the vertices, foci, and asymptotes of <m>16y^2 - 9x^2 = 144</m> and sketch its
      graph.</p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-hyperbola-2">
      <p>Dividing by <m>144</m> and being careful with signs,</p>
      <me>\frac{y^2}{9} - \frac{x^2}{16} = 1.</me>
      <p>
        Now the <em><m>y^2</m>-term</em> is the positive one, so the hyperbola opens
        <em>up and down</em>. In this orientation <m>a^2</m> is the denominator under
        <m>y^2</m>, giving <m>a = 3</m> (measured along the <m>y</m>-axis) and <m>b = 4</m>.
        Still using <m>c^2 = a^2 + b^2</m>,
      </p>
      <me>c^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 \;\Longrightarrow\; c = 5.</me>
      <p>
        So the vertices are <m>(0,\pm 3)</m> and the foci are <m>(0,\pm 5)</m>, both on the
        <m>y</m>-axis. For a vertical hyperbola the asymptotes are
        <m>y = \pm\dfrac{a}{b}\,x = \pm\dfrac{3}{4}x</m><mdash/>note the slope uses
        <m>a/b</m> here, not <m>b/a</m>. The transverse axis has simply rotated from
        horizontal to vertical.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-hyperbola-extra">
        <caption>The vertical hyperbola <m>\frac{y^2}{9}-\frac{x^2}{16}=1</m>: it opens up
        and down, with vertices <m>(0,\pm3)</m>, foci <m>(0,\pm5)</m>, and asymptotes
        <m>y=\pm\frac34 x</m>.</caption>
        <image width="60%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="hyperbola-extra">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,358)" margins="8">
            <definition>H(x,y) = y^2/9 - x^2/16</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-9.5, -8.5, 9.5, 8.5)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1" hticks="(-8,4,8)" vticks="(-8,4,8)" tick-size="(4,4)"/>
              <group at="asymptotes">
                <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(4,3)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
                <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(4,-3)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="5 4" infinite="yes"/>
              </group>
              <implicit-curve at="hyperbola" function="H" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <label at="asym-r" anchor="(6.2,4.65)" alignment="sw"><m>y=\tfrac34 x</m></label>
              <label at="asym-l" anchor="(-6.2,4.65)" alignment="se"><m>y=-\tfrac34 x</m></label>
              <group at="foci">
                <point p="(0,5)" fill="green" size="3"/>
                <point p="(0,-5)" fill="green" size="3"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(0,5)" alignment="e" offset="(8,2)"><m>F_1(0,5)</m></label>
              <label anchor="(0,-5)" alignment="e" offset="(8,-2)"><m>F_2(0,-5)</m></label>
              <group at="vertices">
                <point p="(0,3)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
                <point p="(0,-3)" fill="red" size="2.5"/>
              </group>
              <label at="v1-label" anchor="(0,3)" alignment="e" offset="(8,-12)"><m>V_1</m></label>
              <label at="v2-label" anchor="(0,-3)" alignment="e" offset="(8,12)"><m>V_2</m></label>
              <label at="eqlabel" anchor="(4.6,-6.8)" alignment="center"><m>\frac{y^2}{9}-\frac{x^2}{16}=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="hyperbola"
                  text="The twist example: a vertical hyperbola that opens up and down, so its vertices and foci lie on the y axis">
                <annotation ref="hyperbola"
                    text="The hyperbola y squared over nine minus x squared over sixteen equals one, two blue branches opening up and down" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="asymptotes"
                    text="The dashed asymptotes y equals plus and minus three quarters x, whose slope here is a over b"/>
                <annotation ref="vertices"
                    text="The vertices at zero comma plus and minus three on the y axis, where a equals three"/>
                <annotation ref="foci"
                    text="The foci at zero comma plus and minus five on the y axis, since c squared equals nine plus sixteen equals twenty five"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ============== APPLICATION: PARABOLIC REFLECTOR ============== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-app-reflector">
    <title>Application: Parabolic Reflectors</title>
    <p>
      Satellite dishes, radio telescopes, headlights, and solar cookers are all parabolic in
      cross-section, and for one reason: a parabola has a <term>reflection property</term>.
      Every ray travelling parallel to the axis of the parabola reflects off the curve and
      passes through the <em>focus</em>. Run the argument backwards and a source placed at the
      focus emits a perfectly parallel beam<mdash/>which is why a headlight is shaped this way too.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a coincidence of the shape; it follows from the equidistance definition. For
      <m>x^2 = 4py</m> the tangent at the point <m>(x_0,\,x_0^2/4p)</m> has slope <m>x_0/2p</m>,
      and a short computation shows that the incoming vertical ray and the segment from that
      point to <m>(0,p)</m> make equal angles with the normal. The law of reflection then sends
      every such ray straight to the focus, so that is where the receiver goes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-parabola-reflector">
      <caption>A parabolic dish. Rays arriving parallel to the axis (orange) reflect off the
      dish (red) and all converge on the focus, where the receiver sits.</caption>
      <image width="70%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="parabola-reflector">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,204)" margins="8">
            <definition>pval = 0.6</definition>
            <definition>f(x) = x^2/(4*pval)</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.6, -0.6, 2.6, 2.05)">
              <graph at="dish" function="f" domain="(-2.2, 2.2)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
              <group at="rays">
                <line p1="(-2.0, 2.0)" p2="(-2.0, f(-2.0))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-2.0, f(-2.0))" p2="(0, pval)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-1.2, 2.0)" p2="(-1.2, f(-1.2))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-1.2, f(-1.2))" p2="(0, pval)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(1.2, 2.0)" p2="(1.2, f(1.2))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(1.2, f(1.2))" p2="(0, pval)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(2.0, 2.0)" p2="(2.0, f(2.0))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(2.0, f(2.0))" p2="(0, pval)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
              </group>
              <point at="focus" p="(0,pval)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label at="focus-label" anchor="(0,pval)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-10)"><m>\text{focus (receiver)}</m></label>
              <label at="ray-label" anchor="(0,1.9)" alignment="center"><m>\text{incoming parallel rays}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="dish"
                  text="A parabolic reflector: incoming rays parallel to the axis all reflect to the focus">
                <annotation ref="dish"
                    text="The parabolic cross-section of the dish, a blue upward curve" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="rays"
                    text="Four vertical incoming rays, each striking the dish and reflecting to a single common point"/>
                <annotation ref="focus"
                    text="The focus, where every reflected ray converges; the receiver is placed here"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-parabolic-reflector">
      <caption>Animation: parallel rays strike the dish at different points and all reflect
      through the single focus.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-parabolic-reflector"
             source="videos/parabolic_reflector_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-app-reflector">
      <title>Placing the receiver</title>
      <p>
        A satellite dish has a parabolic cross-section that is <m>4</m> feet wide and <m>1</m>
        foot deep at its center. How far from the vertex should the receiver be mounted?
      </p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-app-reflector">
      <p>
        Put the vertex at the origin with the dish opening upward, so the cross-section is
        <m>x^2 = 4py</m>. The dish is <m>4</m> feet wide and <m>1</m> foot deep, so the rim
        passes through the point <m>(2,1)</m>. Substituting,
      </p>
      <me>2^2 = 4p(1) \;\Longrightarrow\; 4 = 4p \;\Longrightarrow\; p = 1.</me>
      <p>
        The receiver belongs at the focus <m>(0,p) = (0,1)</m>, that is, <m>1</m> foot above the
        vertex<mdash/>which here happens to be exactly level with the rim.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ============== APPLICATION: WHISPERING GALLERY ============== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-app-whisper">
    <title>Application: Whispering Galleries</title>
    <p>
      An ellipse has a reflection property of its own: a ray leaving one focus reflects off the
      ellipse and passes through the <em>other</em> focus. In a room whose ceiling is a
      half-ellipse<mdash/>the dome of St. Paul's Cathedral, or Statuary Hall in the U.S.
      Capitol<mdash/>a whisper released at one focus is gathered up by the whole ceiling and
      delivered to a listener standing at the other focus, while people in between hear nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The constant-sum definition explains why it works so well. Every path from one focus to the
      wall and on to the other focus has total length <m>r_1 + r_2 = 2a</m><mdash/>the
      <em>same</em> length, no matter where it strikes. So the reflected sound does not merely
      arrive at the far focus: it arrives from every direction <em>at the same instant</em>, in
      phase, and the pieces reinforce one another instead of smearing out. That is what makes a
      whisper audible across the room.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-stpauls-1809">
      <caption>The interior of St. Paul's Cathedral beneath Wren's dome. Hand-coloured
      aquatint by Thomas Rowlandson and Augustus Pugin, from Ackermann's
      <pubtitle>Microcosm of London</pubtitle> (1809). Public domain, via
      <url href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/744873">The Metropolitan
      Museum of Art</url>.</caption>
      <image source="images/st-pauls-interior-1809.jpg" width="60%">
        <description>A hand-coloured aquatint from 1809 showing the interior of Saint
        Paul's Cathedral, looking up toward the great dome above the crossing, with
        small figures of visitors walking the marble floor below.</description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ellipse-whisper">
      <caption>A whispering gallery seen from above. Five sound paths leave the speaker at one
      focus, bounce off the elliptical wall, and all arrive at the listener at the other focus.
      Each path has the same total length <m>2a</m>.</caption>
      <image width="70%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="ellipse-whisper">
          <diagram dimensions="(420,277)" margins="8">
            <definition>aa = 3.2</definition>
            <definition>bb = 2.2</definition>
            <definition>cc = sqrt(aa^2 - bb^2)</definition>
            <definition>E(x,y) = x^2/aa^2 + y^2/bb^2</definition>
            <definition>Px(t) = aa*cos(t)</definition>
            <definition>Py(t) = bb*sin(t)</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-4.1, -2.7, 4.1, 2.7)">
              <implicit-curve at="gallery" function="E" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
              <group at="paths">
                <line p1="(-cc,0)" p2="(Px(0.6),Py(0.6))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(Px(0.6),Py(0.6))" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-cc,0)" p2="(Px(1.6),Py(1.6))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(Px(1.6),Py(1.6))" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-cc,0)" p2="(Px(2.5),Py(2.5))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(Px(2.5),Py(2.5))" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-cc,0)" p2="(Px(-2.4),Py(-2.4))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(Px(-2.4),Py(-2.4))" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(-cc,0)" p2="(Px(-0.8),Py(-0.8))" stroke="orange" thickness="1.5"/>
                <line p1="(Px(-0.8),Py(-0.8))" p2="(cc,0)" stroke="red" thickness="1.5"/>
              </group>
              <point at="f2" p="(-cc,0)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label at="f2-label" anchor="(-cc,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-6,-6)"><m>F_2</m></label>
              <label at="f2-role" anchor="(-cc,-2.45)" alignment="center"><m>\text{speaker}</m></label>
              <point at="f1" p="(cc,0)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label at="f1-label" anchor="(cc,0)" alignment="se" offset="(6,-6)"><m>F_1</m></label>
              <label at="f1-role" anchor="(cc,-2.45)" alignment="center"><m>\text{listener}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="gallery"
                  text="A whispering gallery: sound leaving one focus reflects off the elliptical wall and reconverges at the other focus">
                <annotation ref="gallery"
                    text="The elliptical wall of the gallery, seen from above" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="paths"
                    text="Five sound paths leaving the speaker at one focus, striking the wall at different points, and all arriving at the listener at the other focus"/>
                <annotation ref="f2" text="One focus, where the speaker whispers"/>
                <annotation ref="f1" text="The other focus, where the whisper is heard"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="vid-whispering-gallery">
      <caption>Animation: sound leaves one focus in many directions, reflects off the wall, and
      reconverges at the other focus<mdash/>every path the same length <m>2a</m>.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-whispering-gallery"
             source="videos/whispering_gallery_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-app-whisper">
      <title>Where should the listener stand?</title>
      <p>
        A whispering gallery is <m>100</m> feet long and <m>60</m> feet wide. Where should the
        speaker and the listener stand, and how far does the whisper travel on its way across?
      </p>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-app-whisper">
      <p>
        Model the room as <m>\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1</m> with center at the
        origin. The room is <m>100</m> feet long and <m>60</m> feet wide, so <m>2a = 100</m> and
        <m>2b = 60</m>, giving <m>a = 50</m> and <m>b = 30</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me>c^2 = a^2 - b^2 = 2500 - 900 = 1600 \;\Longrightarrow\; c = 40.</me>
      <p>
        The two people should stand at the foci <m>(\pm 40, 0)</m>, that is, <m>40</m> feet from
        the center along the long axis, or <m>80</m> feet apart. Whatever point of the wall the
        sound bounces off, it travels a total of <m>r_1 + r_2 = 2a = 100</m> feet<mdash/>the same
        distance every way round, which is exactly why it arrives in phase.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </subsection>

    <!-- ============== APPLICATION: HYPERBOLIC NAVIGATION ============== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-app-loran">
    <title>Application: Hyperbolic Navigation (LORAN)</title>
    <p>
      Progress in electrical engineering enabled navigation systems based on the transmission
      of electromagnetic waves. One example is LORAN-C marine navigation, developed in the
      United States during World War II. A vessel receives a synchronized signal from a pair
      of transmitters; the signal from the more distant transmitter arrives later, so the
      measured delay <m>\Delta t</m> determines the <em>difference</em> between the vessel's
      distances to the two transmitters,
    </p>
    <me>|r_2 - r_1| = v\,\Delta t,</me>
    <p>
      where <m>v</m> is the signal speed. The set of points with a constant difference of
      distances from two fixed points is precisely a hyperbola with those points as foci, so
      one measurement tells the vessel which hyperbola it is on<mdash/>the one with
      <m>2a = v\,\Delta t</m><mdash/>and the sign of the delay (which pulse arrived first)
      tells it which branch. A delay measured against another pair of stations places the
      vessel on a second curve, and since its true position must satisfy both measurements at
      once, the vessel sits where the two curves cross.
    </p>

<figure xml:id="fig-hyperbola-loran">
      <caption>Hyperbolic navigation. The measured delay places the unknown position <m>A</m> on the
      solid hyperbola with foci at the receivers <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_2</m>; a different delay
      would place it on the dashed one instead.</caption>
      <image width="65%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="hyperbola-loran">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,320)" margins="8">
            <definition>cc = 3</definition>
            <definition>aa = 1.5</definition>
            <definition>bb = sqrt(cc^2 - aa^2)</definition>
            <definition>H(x,y) = x^2/aa^2 - y^2/bb^2</definition>
            <definition>aa2 = 0.7</definition>
            <definition>bb2 = sqrt(cc^2 - aa2^2)</definition>
            <definition>H2(x,y) = x^2/aa2^2 - y^2/bb2^2</definition>
            <definition>sP = 0.9</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-5.0, -4.0, 5.0, 4.0)">
              <implicit-curve at="locus2" function="H2" k="1" stroke="gray" thickness="1.5" dash="4 4"/>
              <implicit-curve at="locus" function="H" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
              <group at="signals">
                <line p1="(-cc,0)" p2="(aa*cosh(sP), bb*sinh(sP))" stroke="orange" thickness="2"/>
                <line p1="(cc,0)" p2="(aa*cosh(sP), bb*sinh(sP))" stroke="green" thickness="2"/>
              </group>
              <point at="ship" p="(aa*cosh(sP), bb*sinh(sP))" fill="red" size="4"/>
              <label at="ship-label" anchor="(aa*cosh(sP), bb*sinh(sP))" alignment="ne" offset="(6,2)"><m>A</m></label>
              <point at="s2" p="(-cc,0)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label at="sB-label" anchor="(-cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(-6,-8)"><m>P_2</m></label>
              <point at="s1" p="(cc,0)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label at="sA-label" anchor="(cc,0)" alignment="s" offset="(4,-8)"><m>P_1</m></label>
              <label at="locus-label" anchor="(3.75,-2.6)" alignment="center"><m>|r_2-r_1|=2a</m></label>
              <label at="r2-label" anchor="(-1.2,0.93)" alignment="n" offset="(0,6)"><m>r_2</m></label>
              <label at="r1-label" anchor="(2.586,1.3)" alignment="e" offset="(6,0)"><m>r_1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="locus"
                  text="Hyperbolic navigation: a fixed difference in signal arrival times from two receivers places the unknown position A on a hyperbola whose foci are the receivers">
                <annotation ref="locus"
                    text="The hyperbola of all positions having the measured time difference from receivers P one and P two" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="locus2"
                    text="A second dashed hyperbola, the locus for a different time difference, showing that each delay picks out its own curve"/>
                <annotation ref="signals"
                    text="Two signal paths, of length r one from receiver P one and length r two from receiver P two, meeting at the point A"/>
                <annotation ref="ship" text="The unknown position A, somewhere on the locus, closer to P one"/>
                <annotation ref="s1" text="Receiver P one, the nearer focus"/>
                <annotation ref="s2" text="Receiver P two, the farther focus"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

<figure xml:id="vid-hyperbolic-navigation">
      <caption>Animation: the point <m>A</m> moves, and both signal distances <m>r_1</m> and
      <m>r_2</m> change<mdash/>but their difference does not, tracing out the hyperbola.</caption>
      <video xml:id="video-hyperbolic-navigation"
             source="videos/hyperbolic_navigation_1080p.mp4"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-app-loran">
      <title>Locating a hiker from three receivers</title>
      <p>
        Three receivers <m>P_1</m>, <m>P_2</m>, and <m>P_3</m> are deployed in the landscape.
        The figure captures the distances we know. Emma's tourist navigation device sends a
        signal to all three receivers. The signal arrives at receivers <m>P_1</m> and
        <m>P_3</m> at the same time, and at receiver <m>P_2</m> it arrives
        <m>80</m> microseconds later. Where is Emma located? Assume the signal travels
        <m>300{,}000</m> km per second, and determine the position in a suitably chosen
        coordinate system.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-loran-assignment">
        <caption>The exercise data: <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_2</m> are <m>48</m> km apart, and
        <m>P_3</m> is <m>36</m> km directly above the point <m>12</m> km beyond
        <m>P_1</m>.</caption>
        <image width="75%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="loran-assignment">
          <diagram dimensions="(420,255)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(-8, -9, 76, 42)">
              <line at="baseline" p1="(0,0)" p2="(60,0)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3 3"/>
              <line at="riser" p1="(60,0)" p2="(60,36)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3 3"/>
              <group at="right-angle">
                <line p1="(57,0)" p2="(57,3)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(57,3)" p2="(60,3)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
              </group>
              <group at="dist-48">
                <line p1="(0,-5)" p2="(48,-5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(0,-6.5)" p2="(0,-3.5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(48,-6.5)" p2="(48,-3.5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(24,-5)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)"><m>48\ \text{km}</m></label>
              <group at="dist-12">
                <line p1="(48,-5)" p2="(60,-5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(60,-6.5)" p2="(60,-3.5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(54,-5)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)"><m>12\ \text{km}</m></label>
              <group at="dist-36">
                <line p1="(65,0)" p2="(65,36)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(63.5,0)" p2="(66.5,0)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(63.5,36)" p2="(66.5,36)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
              </group>
              <label anchor="(65,18)" alignment="e" offset="(5,0)"><m>36\ \text{km}</m></label>
              <point at="p2" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="n" offset="(0,7)"><m>P_2</m></label>
              <point at="p1" p="(48,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(48,0)" alignment="n" offset="(0,7)"><m>P_1</m></label>
              <point at="p3" p="(60,36)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(60,36)" alignment="w" offset="(-7,0)"><m>P_3</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="p1"
                  text="The exercise data: three receivers in the landscape with the known distances between them">
                <annotation ref="p2" text="Receiver P two, at the left end of a horizontal dotted line"/>
                <annotation ref="p1" text="Receiver P one, on the same horizontal line, forty eight kilometers to the right of P two"/>
                <annotation ref="p3" text="Receiver P three, thirty six kilometers directly above the point twelve kilometers to the right of P one"/>
                <annotation ref="right-angle" text="A small square marking the right angle where the vertical dotted line meets the horizontal one"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </example>

    <solution xml:id="sol-app-loran">
      <p>
        We first translate the two timing facts into geometry. The signal reaches <m>P_1</m>
        and <m>P_3</m> simultaneously, so Emma is <em>equidistant</em> from them: he lies on
        the perpendicular bisector of the segment <m>P_1P_3</m>. The signal reaches
        <m>P_2</m> <m>80</m> microseconds after <m>P_1</m>, so Emma is
      </p>
      <me>(300{,}000\ \text{km/s})\,(80\times 10^{-6}\ \text{s}) = 24\ \text{km}</me>
      <p>
        farther from <m>P_2</m> than from <m>P_1</m>. He therefore also lies on the branch
        nearer <m>P_1</m> of the hyperbola <m>h</m> with foci <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_2</m> whose
        constant distance difference is <m>24</m> km.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now we choose coordinates so that <m>h</m> has the simplest possible equation. Place
        the origin <m>O</m> at the midpoint of <m>P_1P_2</m>, point the positive
        <m>x</m>-axis along <m>OP_1</m>, and choose the positive <m>y</m>-axis so that
        <m>P_3</m> has positive second coordinate. Since every given length is a multiple of
        <m>12</m>, let one unit on each axis be <m>12</m> km. Then
      </p>
      <me>P_1(2,0), \qquad P_2(-2,0), \qquad P_3(3,3).</me>

      <figure xml:id="fig-loran-coords">
        <caption>The coordinate system: origin at the midpoint of <m>P_1P_2</m>, one unit
        <m>= 12</m> km.</caption>
        <image width="65%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="loran-coords">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,271)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.9, -0.9, 3.9, 3.7)">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
              <line at="drop-v" p1="(3,0)" p2="(3,3)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3 3"/>
              <line at="drop-h" p1="(0,3)" p2="(3,3)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3 3"/>
              <point at="origin" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="2"/>
              <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-4,-4)"><m>O</m></label>
              <point at="p2" p="(-2,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(-2,0)" alignment="n" offset="(0,7)"><m>P_2</m></label>
              <point at="p1" p="(2,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(2,0)" alignment="n" offset="(-6,7)"><m>P_1</m></label>
              <point at="p3" p="(3,3)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(3,3)" alignment="se" offset="(6,-4)"><m>P_3</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="p1"
                  text="The chosen coordinate system, in units of twelve kilometers">
                <annotation ref="origin" text="The origin O at the midpoint of segment P one P two"/>
                <annotation ref="p2" text="Receiver P two at minus two comma zero"/>
                <annotation ref="p1" text="Receiver P one at two comma zero"/>
                <annotation ref="p3" text="Receiver P three at three comma three"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> denote Emma's unknown position, and let <m>o</m> be the perpendicular
        bisector of <m>P_1P_3</m>. The line <m>o</m> passes through the midpoint
        <m>S\left(\tfrac52, \tfrac32\right)</m> of the segment, and since
        <m>P_3 - P_1 = (1,3)</m>, the direction <m>\vec u_o = (3,-1)</m> is perpendicular to
        the segment. Parametrically,
      </p>
      <me>o:\quad x = \tfrac52 + 3t, \qquad y = \tfrac32 - t, \qquad t \in \mathbb{R}.</me>
      <p>
        For the hyperbola, the foci are <m>P_1(2,0)</m> and <m>P_2(-2,0)</m>, so the center is
        <m>O</m> and <m>c = |OP_1| = 2</m>. The constant difference
        <m>|AP_2| - |AP_1| = 24\ \text{km} = 2</m> units equals <m>2a</m>, so <m>a = 1</m>,
        and then <m>b^2 = c^2 - a^2 = 4 - 1 = 3</m>. Hence
      </p>
      <me>h:\quad x^2 - \frac{y^2}{3} = 1,</me>
      <p>
        and since <m>A</m> is closer to <m>P_1</m>, it lies on the right branch:
        <m>x_A \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        It remains to intersect <m>o</m> with <m>h</m>. Substituting the parametric equations
        into <m>3x^2 - y^2 = 3</m>:
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>3\left(\tfrac52 + 3t\right)^2 - \left(\tfrac32 - t\right)^2 \amp= 3</mrow>
        <mrow>3\left(\tfrac{25}{4} + 15t + 9t^2\right) - \left(\tfrac94 - 3t + t^2\right) \amp= 3</mrow>
        <mrow>26t^2 + 48t + \tfrac{27}{2} \amp= 0</mrow>
        <mrow>52t^2 + 96t + 27 \amp= 0.</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        The discriminant is <m>96^2 - 4\cdot 52\cdot 27 = 9216 - 5616 = 3600 = 60^2</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>t = \frac{-96 \pm 60}{104}, \qquad t_1 = -\frac{9}{26}, \quad t_2 = -\frac32.</me>
      <p>Substituting <m>t_1</m> into the parametric equations gives</p>
      <me>x_1 = \tfrac52 + 3\left(-\tfrac{9}{26}\right) = \tfrac{19}{13}, \qquad
          y_1 = \tfrac32 - \left(-\tfrac{9}{26}\right) = \tfrac{24}{13},</me>
      <p>
        that is, <m>A_1\left(\tfrac{19}{13}, \tfrac{24}{13}\right)</m>. Substituting
        <m>t_2</m> gives <m>x_2 = \tfrac52 - \tfrac92 = -2</m> and
        <m>y_2 = \tfrac32 + \tfrac32 = 3</m>, that is, <m>A_2(-2,3)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point <m>A_2</m> fails the branch condition <m>x_A \gt 0</m>, and the physics
        says why: <m>A_2</m> lies on the <em>left</em> branch, <m>24</m> km closer to
        <m>P_2</m> than to <m>P_1</m>, so a signal sent from <m>A_2</m> would reach
        <m>P_2</m> <em>earlier</em> than <m>P_1</m><mdash/>the opposite of what was measured.
        Emma's position is therefore
      </p>
      <me>A\left(\tfrac{19}{13},\ \tfrac{24}{13}\right),</me>
      <p>
        about <m>(17.5,\ 22.2)</m> in kilometers from <m>O</m>. As a check, the distances come
        out exactly: <m>|AP_1| = |AP_3| = \tfrac{25}{13}</m> units and
        <m>|AP_2| = \tfrac{51}{13}</m> units, so <m>|AP_2| - |AP_1| = 2</m> units
        <m>= 24</m> km, as required. In kilometers, Emma is
        <m>\tfrac{300}{13} \approx 23.1</m> km from <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_3</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-loran-solution">
        <caption>The solution: <m>A</m> is the intersection of the bisector <m>o</m> with the
        right branch of <m>h</m>. The second intersection <m>A_2(-2,3)</m>, on the left
        branch, is rejected.</caption>
        <image width="80%">
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="loran-solution">
          <diagram dimensions="(440,253)" margins="8">
            <definition>H(x,y) = x^2 - y^2/3</definition>
            <definition>Ax = 19/13</definition>
            <definition>Ay = 24/13</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-4.4, -1.3, 4.8, 4.0)">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
              <implicit-curve at="hyp" function="H" k="1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <line at="axis-o" p1="(-4.1,3.7)" p2="(4.6,0.8)" stroke="black" thickness="1.5"/>
              <label anchor="(4.6,0.8)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,4)"><m>o</m></label>
              <label at="hyp-label" anchor="(1.85,3.4)" alignment="w" offset="(-2,0)"><m>h</m></label>
              <line at="drop-v" p1="(Ax,0)" p2="(Ax,Ay)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3 3"/>
              <line at="drop-h" p1="(0,Ay)" p2="(Ax,Ay)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3 3"/>
              <label anchor="(Ax,0)" alignment="s" offset="(4,-4)"><m>\tfrac{19}{13}</m></label>
              <label at="lab-2413" anchor="(0,Ay)" alignment="w" offset="(-4,-9)"><m>\tfrac{24}{13}</m></label>
              <point at="ptA" p="(Ax,Ay)" fill="red" size="3.5"/>
              <label anchor="(Ax,Ay)" alignment="ne" offset="(5,3)"><m>A</m></label>
              <point at="ptA2" p="(-2,3)" fill="white" size="3"/>
              <label at="a2-label" anchor="(-2,3)" alignment="sw" offset="(-6,-6)"><m>A_2\ \text{(rejected)}</m></label>
              <point at="ptS" p="(2.5,1.5)" fill="black" size="2.5"/>
              <label at="s-label" anchor="(2.5,1.5)" alignment="n" offset="(6,6)"><m>S</m></label>
              <point at="p2" p="(-2,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label at="p2-label" anchor="(-2,0)" alignment="s" offset="(-16,-7)"><m>P_2</m></label>
              <point at="p1" p="(2,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label at="p1-label" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="s" offset="(16,-7)"><m>P_1</m></label>
              <point at="p3" p="(3,3)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label anchor="(3,3)" alignment="e" offset="(6,0)"><m>P_3</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="hyp"
                  text="The solution: Emma sits at the crossing of the perpendicular bisector of P one P three and the right branch of the hyperbola with foci P one and P two">
                <annotation ref="hyp" text="The hyperbola h, x squared minus y squared over three equals one, with two branches" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="axis-o" text="The line o, the perpendicular bisector of segment P one P three, passing through its midpoint S"/>
                <annotation ref="ptA" text="Emma's position A at nineteen thirteenths comma twenty four thirteenths, on the right branch"/>
                <annotation ref="ptA2" text="The rejected second intersection A two at minus two comma three, on the left branch"/>
                <annotation ref="ptS" text="The midpoint S of segment P one P three"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>

    <remark xml:id="rem-loran-two-hyperbolas">
      <title>What if Emma were not equidistant from <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_3</m>?</title>
      <p>
        The exercise was arranged so that the signal reached <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_3</m>
        simultaneously, and that is what kept the algebra easy: a distance difference of
        <em>zero</em> is the degenerate case of the hyperbola, and the locus collapses to a
        straight line, the perpendicular bisector of <m>P_1P_3</m>. We then only had to
        substitute a linear parametrization into one quadratic equation.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the two arrival times differed, the delay would instead give a <em>nonzero</em>
        difference <m>\bigl||AP_3| - |AP_1|\bigr| = v\,\Delta t</m>, and Emma would lie on a
        genuine second hyperbola, with foci <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_3</m><mdash/>tilted, since its
        focal axis <m>P_1P_3</m> is not parallel to a coordinate axis. Locating him would then
        mean intersecting the correct branches of <em>two</em> hyperbolas, as in the figure
        below. Conceptually nothing changes<mdash/>each measurement contributes one curve, and
        the position is their common point<mdash/>but algebraically we would be solving two
        quadratic equations together rather than a linear one and a quadratic one.
      </p>
    </remark>

<figure xml:id="fig-hyperbola-loran-fix">
      <caption>Two measurements fix the position. The first delay (receivers <m>P_1</m>,
      <m>P_2</m>) puts <m>A</m> on the blue branch; the second (receivers <m>P_1</m>,
      <m>P_3</m>) puts it on the purple branch. The position <m>A</m> is at the crossing,
      and the dashed segments show the three distances <m>r_1</m>, <m>r_2</m>,
      <m>r_3</m>.</caption>
      <image width="65%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="hyperbola-loran-fix">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,324)" margins="8">
            <definition>sP = 0.9</definition>
            <definition>Px = 1.5*cosh(sP)</definition>
            <definition>Py = sqrt(6.75)*sinh(sP)</definition>
            <definition>k1 = sqrt((Px+3)^2 + Py^2) - sqrt((Px-3)^2 + Py^2)</definition>
            <definition>k2 = sqrt((Px+1)^2 + (Py-3.8)^2) - sqrt((Px-3)^2 + Py^2)</definition>
            <definition>H1(x,y) = sqrt((x+3)^2 + y^2) - sqrt((x-3)^2 + y^2)</definition>
            <definition>H2(x,y) = sqrt((x+1)^2 + (y-3.8)^2) - sqrt((x-3)^2 + y^2)</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-5.0, -3.5, 5.0, 4.6)">
              <implicit-curve at="locus1" function="H1" k="k1" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
              <implicit-curve at="locus2" function="H2" k="k2" stroke="purple" thickness="2.5"/>
              <group at="dists">
                <line p1="(Px,Py)" p2="(3,0)" stroke="black" thickness="1.2" dash="4 3"/>
                <line p1="(Px,Py)" p2="(-3,0)" stroke="black" thickness="1.2" dash="4 3"/>
                <line p1="(Px,Py)" p2="(-1,3.8)" stroke="black" thickness="1.2" dash="4 3"/>
                <label at="r1-label" anchor="(2.586,1.3)" alignment="e" offset="(6,0)"><m>r_1</m></label>
                <label at="r2-label" anchor="(-1.2,0.93)" alignment="n" offset="(0,6)"><m>r_2</m></label>
                <label at="r3-label" anchor="(0.57,3.23)" alignment="n" offset="(0,5)"><m>r_3</m></label>
              </group>
              <group at="legend">
                <line p1="(-4.9,3.1)" p2="(-1.75,3.1)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(-4.9,4.5)" p2="(-1.75,4.5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(-4.9,3.1)" p2="(-4.9,4.5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(-1.75,3.1)" p2="(-1.75,4.5)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <line p1="(-4.75,4.1)" p2="(-4.45,4.1)" stroke="blue" thickness="2.5"/>
                <line p1="(-4.75,3.5)" p2="(-4.45,3.5)" stroke="purple" thickness="2.5"/>
                <label at="leg1" anchor="(-4.35,4.1)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)"><m>r_2-r_1=2a</m></label>
                <label at="leg2" anchor="(-4.35,3.5)" alignment="e" offset="(4,0)"><m>r_3-r_1=2a'</m></label>
              </group>
              <point at="ship" p="(Px,Py)" fill="red" size="4"/>
              <label at="ship-label" anchor="(Px,Py)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)"><m>A</m></label>
              <point at="sA" p="(3,0)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="se" offset="(2,-6)"><m>P_1</m></label>
              <point at="sB" p="(-3,0)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label anchor="(-3,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-2,-6)"><m>P_2</m></label>
              <point at="sC" p="(-1,3.8)" fill="black" size="4"/>
              <label anchor="(-1,3.8)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,2)"><m>P_3</m></label>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="locus1"
                  text="A position fix from two time-difference measurements: the unknown position A lies at the crossing of two hyperbola branches">
                <annotation ref="locus1"
                    text="The blue branch of points whose distance difference to receivers P two and P one equals the first measured constant" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="locus2"
                    text="The purple branch of points whose distance difference to receivers P three and P one equals the second measured constant" sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="dists"
                    text="Three dashed segments from the position A to the receivers, of lengths r one, r two, and r three"/>
                <annotation ref="legend"
                    text="A legend box in the upper left: on the blue curve, r two minus r one equals two a; on the purple curve, r three minus r one equals two a prime"/>
                <annotation ref="ship" text="The position A, at the single crossing of the two branches"/>
                <annotation ref="sA" text="Receiver P one, the master receiver shared by both measurements"/>
                <annotation ref="sB" text="Receiver P two, paired with P one for the first measurement"/>
                <annotation ref="sC" text="Receiver P three, paired with P one for the second measurement"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

</section>
